00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:02.193
So, you know, in our bodies,

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:04.770
there's kind of a...

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:09.193
Let's see if this is gonna
start drawing or not.

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:12.040
I think I'm gonna have to use my finger.

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:12.960
Okay, there it is.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:16.600
Yeah, so in our bodies there's
kind of a constant balance

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.750
between cell growth and cell death.

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:21.850
So here I'm kind of just drawing...

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:25.913
If you were to look at your
skin under a microscope,

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:30.947
you would see you kind of
a nice balance of cells

00:04:30.947 --> 00:04:33.580
that kind of grow up from the bottom.

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:34.910
We call it basement membrane,

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:36.680
which is down here.

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:39.913
And then as they grow up
in layers, they die off.

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:45.410
So when it's dried out like now,

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:46.310
you're kind of ashy,

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:49.543
that's all those dead skin
cells that are coming off.

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:52.570
So there's a balance between
these cells at the bottom

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:53.403
that have the grow,

00:04:53.403 --> 00:04:55.463
and the cells at the top that have to die.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:58.030
So if you lose this balance,

00:04:58.030 --> 00:05:00.160
then you can just keep
growing these cells,

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:02.420
and maybe you'd be walking around

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:04.080
with six inches of skin,

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:06.903
which would not be very good to live with.

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:09.650
So this is what's happening in our bodies

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:10.640
every day in every tissue.

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:13.830
There's a constant balance
between cells that are dying

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:17.613
and cells that are growing in
order to maintain our tissue.

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:21.173
So what happens in cancer?

00:05:22.098 --> 00:05:23.643
I'm just gonna make a new one here.

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:30.470
So in cancer, instead of
having this nice, flat,

00:05:30.470 --> 00:05:33.040
even layer of cells,

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:37.770
you got a cell that kind of
goes wild and keeps growing,

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:42.853
disrupts all the nice
even architecture we have,

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:46.980
and then keeps growing.

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:51.393
And that's what we call a tumor.

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:56.893
And how does this imbalance
happen in that cell?

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:01.760
So what happens is that a cell

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:06.620
has to take on what we call it a mutation.

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:09.519
So here's an individual cell,

00:06:09.519 --> 00:06:12.963
and we're just gonna simplify
its DNA in the nucleus here.

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:16.200
So what has to happen for
a cell to become cancerous

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:19.050
and start growing crazy and never dying,

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:22.280
is it has to have these
mutations in its genome,

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:27.280
and that's DNA that turns off a gene

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:29.490
that stopped cell growth.

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:31.640
And that's what we call
a tumor suppressor.

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:34.010
So tumor suppressor is a gene that,

00:06:34.010 --> 00:06:35.810
when it functions normally,

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:39.340
stops yourselves from growing too crazily.

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:42.000
So when you have any mutation
in a tumor suppressor,

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:43.410
then the cell kind of...

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:46.880
You're kind of cutting
the brakes on that cell

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:48.210
and it's able to keep growing,

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:49.790
even at the conditions

00:06:49.790 --> 00:06:52.680
normally it wouldn't be
right for it to grow.

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:55.100
But you have to be unlucky
to also get a mutation,

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:57.630
and what we call an oncogene.

00:06:57.630 --> 00:07:00.473
So I'm just gonna try to write this out.

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:07.450
So an oncogene is a gene
that makes a cell grow,

00:07:07.450 --> 00:07:09.560
and in normal conditions,
it's not always on,

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:12.130
because, again, you don't
want the cells in your body

00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:13.970
to be constantly growing.

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:15.670
But if you're unlucky
enough to get a mutation

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:18.180
in both a tumor suppressor
and an oncogene,

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:21.010
then the cell has no
limitations to its growth.

00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:22.500
So you're basically both cutting to breaks

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:25.780
and putting a brick on the gas,

00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:30.043
and the cancer cell just
takes off and forms a tumor.

