00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:04.550
so very good.

00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:08.620
So it is possible to
be born with a mutation

00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:10.720
in one of these genes.

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:14.603
So an example of that you may
have heard of is called BRCA.

00:10:16.490 --> 00:10:19.410
That was one thing that
Angelina Jolie had,

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:23.130
and she got what we call
a preventative mastectomy.

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:25.770
So she surgically removed her breasts

00:10:25.770 --> 00:10:28.280
to prevent the chance of
her getting breast cancer,

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:30.930
because she was born with
a mutation in the gene

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:34.930
that could increase your chances
of getting breast cancer,

00:10:34.930 --> 00:10:36.253
as well as ovarian cancer.

00:10:37.430 --> 00:10:39.530
So very few of you chose oxygen,

00:10:39.530 --> 00:10:40.760
and that's kind of a trick question,

00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:44.020
but actually oxygen can cause cancers.

00:10:44.020 --> 00:10:48.150
So what happens in our
cells is that oxygen

00:10:48.150 --> 00:10:51.120
can cause these things
called free radicals.

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:55.510
And our body has a system in
order to kind of clear oxygen

00:10:55.510 --> 00:10:58.610
that kind of changes its structure

00:10:58.610 --> 00:11:02.110
and can destroy or affect our DNA.

00:11:02.110 --> 00:11:04.340
So when you're actively breathing oxygen,

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:05.960
like, no, that's not gonna
instantly give you cancer,

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:08.260
but if the process within your cell

00:11:08.260 --> 00:11:10.150
can no longer clear these free radicals,

00:11:10.150 --> 00:11:11.500
then it can lead to cancer.

00:11:12.470 --> 00:11:14.383
And some of you guys chose 5G network.

00:11:15.756 --> 00:11:17.170
It actually doesn't cause cancer.

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:20.160
It's just your normal wireless network.

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:22.660
So I know there are probably
a lot of conspiracy theories

00:11:22.660 --> 00:11:23.580
out there about 5G,

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:25.640
but 5G does not cause cancer,

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:27.193
so don't worry about that.

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:29.763
All right.

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:35.980
And, you know, as you grow older,

00:11:35.980 --> 00:11:38.870
the chances of getting
mutations like this increase.

00:11:38.870 --> 00:11:41.290
So that's why most people
with cancer tend to be older,

00:11:41.290 --> 00:11:43.850
just over time there's
more and more chances

00:11:43.850 --> 00:11:46.703
of you to acquire these
kinds of mutations.

00:11:55.380 --> 00:11:58.373
Just gonna make a new canvas again.

00:11:59.610 --> 00:12:01.100
So if you have a tumor

00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:03.283
that's forming in your skin, for example,

00:12:04.590 --> 00:12:06.050
as long as it stays there in the skin,

00:12:06.050 --> 00:12:06.883
it's pretty harmless,

00:12:06.883 --> 00:12:08.560
because there's nothing really around.

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:10.120
So if you had a tumor in your arm,

00:12:10.120 --> 00:12:11.860
there's not really anything around there

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:14.920
that it can affect much.

00:12:14.920 --> 00:12:19.920
So the problem is when these
tumors start to metastasize.

00:12:21.270 --> 00:12:25.423
So I'm again gonna draw my skin here.

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:29.913
And now my cancer is forming.

00:12:32.920 --> 00:12:34.880
So over the course of years and years,

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:37.853
this tumor is developing
and growing bigger.

00:12:38.690 --> 00:12:43.460
And then eventually a few
of the cells in the tumor

00:12:44.660 --> 00:12:48.293
leave the skin and actually
enter the bloodstream.

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:56.490
And once these cells enter the bloodstream

00:12:56.490 --> 00:12:58.240
and kind of look around the body

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:00.490
and see, oh, what would
be a nice environment

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:02.553
for me to go into it and stick around in,

00:13:03.400 --> 00:13:07.093
that's when we have a
problem called metastasis.

00:13:11.060 --> 00:13:11.893
For example,

00:13:16.778 --> 00:13:18.040
say these cancer cells go around

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:20.480
and then they find the lung,

00:13:20.480 --> 00:13:23.380
and they think, hey, it looks
like a pretty good place

00:13:23.380 --> 00:13:24.680
for me to go.

00:13:24.680 --> 00:13:25.590
So they, once again,

00:13:25.590 --> 00:13:27.810
exit the bloodstream,

00:13:27.810 --> 00:13:31.055
and travel to the tissue of the lung.

00:13:31.055 --> 00:13:34.993
And then there they
form yet another tumor.

00:13:37.030 --> 00:13:39.720
And this is what we actually
call the metastasis.

00:13:39.720 --> 00:13:44.720
So a metastasis is a cancer
that has left a primary site,

00:13:45.010 --> 00:13:48.640
so the primary site being
this part of the skin here.

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:51.590
It traveled all over the
body and found and organ,

00:13:51.590 --> 00:13:54.380
a new home for itself to grow in.

00:13:54.380 --> 00:13:57.140
And so this is actually
what kills the majority

00:13:57.140 --> 00:13:59.480
of cancer patients.

00:13:59.480 --> 00:14:02.210
So 90% of cancer patients
die when their tumor

00:14:02.210 --> 00:14:05.480
goes to an organ like the
lung, the brain, the liver,

00:14:05.480 --> 00:14:06.313
and grows there,

00:14:06.313 --> 00:14:10.300
and starts affecting the ability
of that organ to function.

00:14:10.300 --> 00:14:11.600
So when you can no longer breathe,

00:14:11.600 --> 00:14:15.290
when you can no longer perform tasks

00:14:15.290 --> 00:14:17.690
when your brain or your liver
can no longer clear toxins

00:14:17.690 --> 00:14:20.170
because there's tumors growing in it,

00:14:20.170 --> 00:14:23.170
that's how patients
tend to die from cancer.

00:14:23.170 --> 00:14:24.750
So our lab is really trying to figure out

00:14:24.750 --> 00:14:28.650
what causes these cells to
like change their behavior

00:14:28.650 --> 00:14:31.240
and be able to survive

00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:32.900
in a completely different environment

00:14:32.900 --> 00:14:34.900
from where they started.

00:14:34.900 --> 00:14:36.560
And every step of this pathway,

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:38.680
from leaving the primary site,

00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:40.320
to entering the bloodstream,

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:41.290
to leaving the bloodstream,

00:14:41.290 --> 00:14:45.510
to like finding a new organ
and growing in the organ,

00:14:45.510 --> 00:14:47.310
there's only a small percentage of cells

00:14:47.310 --> 00:14:49.663
that can survive each of these steps.

00:14:50.690 --> 00:14:53.040
So I'll have another
question for you guys.

00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:55.340
And, again, you don't
have to be right at all.

00:14:55.340 --> 00:14:56.423
So just take a guess.

00:15:01.983 --> 00:15:05.700
What percentage of cells that
you think are able to make it

00:15:05.700 --> 00:15:09.853
from a bloodstream to actually
forming this metastasis?

00:15:16.760 --> 00:15:19.600
So like what percentage
of cells make it from here

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:21.533
to actually doing this at the end?

00:15:42.910 --> 00:15:46.660
Again, I'll give it
another 15 seconds or so.

00:16:03.118 --> 00:16:03.951
Yeah, I'm just seeing the chat now.

00:16:03.951 --> 00:16:06.950
And you guys talking
about pineapple on pizza.

00:16:06.950 --> 00:16:08.123
I didn't do it either.

00:16:10.520 --> 00:16:13.800
Okay, so almost all of
you voted, so that's good.

00:16:13.800 --> 00:16:14.780
All right. So let's go through.

00:16:14.780 --> 00:16:17.440
So the majority of you guys voted 10%,

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:21.220
which is a very logical guess.

00:16:21.220 --> 00:16:22.600
It seems like small enough,

00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:24.610
but also you would think cells

00:16:24.610 --> 00:16:26.700
that have already made it this far,

00:16:26.700 --> 00:16:29.270
they can probably make it towards the end.

00:16:29.270 --> 00:16:31.770
But the actual answer is 0.01%,

00:16:31.770 --> 00:16:33.533
which is 1/10000.

00:16:34.750 --> 00:16:38.580
So only 1/10000 of all the cells
make it to the bloodstream.

00:16:38.580 --> 00:16:40.500
And, again, that's already
a very small percentage

00:16:40.500 --> 00:16:44.300
of the cells that actually
leave the main tumor.

00:16:44.300 --> 00:16:45.930
Only one of 10,000 of them

00:16:45.930 --> 00:16:48.940
are actually capable of going to the lung,

00:16:48.940 --> 00:16:49.773
or whatever organ,

00:16:49.773 --> 00:16:51.533
and growing again there.

00:16:52.480 --> 00:16:54.660
So, you know, cancer is a constant battle

00:16:54.660 --> 00:16:58.560
of like evolution and numbers.

00:16:58.560 --> 00:17:00.003
You know, the numbers of cells

00:17:00.003 --> 00:17:01.510
that can actually form the metastases

00:17:01.510 --> 00:17:03.090
is very, very small,

00:17:03.090 --> 00:17:06.370
and they have to somehow
evolve a number of techniques

00:17:06.370 --> 00:17:08.120
in order to make it to the end.

00:17:08.120 --> 00:17:12.170
And so, again, that's kind of what our...

00:17:12.170 --> 00:17:13.020
Wait, I forgot to share.

00:17:13.020 --> 00:17:15.490
I don't know if you guys
can see the answer. Yeah.

00:17:15.490 --> 00:17:17.240
So that's, again, what our lab

00:17:17.240 --> 00:17:18.270
is kind of trying to figure out,

00:17:18.270 --> 00:17:21.900
how these cells are able to
pick up like little tricks

00:17:21.900 --> 00:17:23.520
and techniques in order
to actually make it

00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:28.393
to the end of the metastatic
cascade, as we call it.

00:17:30.130 --> 00:17:34.860
Okay, so I'm gonna demonstrate to you guys

00:17:34.860 --> 00:17:37.410
how we actually study this in the lab.

00:17:37.410 --> 00:17:42.410
And to really study this process in a way

00:17:43.630 --> 00:17:45.350
that could be most informative,

00:17:45.350 --> 00:17:47.530
we have to develop a system

00:17:47.530 --> 00:17:49.570
that mimics kind of all these steps

00:17:50.920 --> 00:17:52.480
of both having the primary tumor,

00:17:52.480 --> 00:17:53.733
and then tumor cells

00:17:53.733 --> 00:17:56.330
that are able to get into the bloodstream,

00:17:56.330 --> 00:17:58.260
and then make it to a different organ.

00:17:58.260 --> 00:17:59.850
So basically what immunofluorescence is,

00:17:59.850 --> 00:18:01.550
it's a way of using

00:18:06.050 --> 00:18:10.080
antibodies in order to amplify

00:18:11.190 --> 00:18:13.461
a certain thing that you want to look out.

00:18:13.461 --> 00:18:17.680
So assuming these are all
cancer cells this time,

00:18:17.680 --> 00:18:20.530
these cancer cells have certain proteins

00:18:20.530 --> 00:18:22.580
on their surface that are unique to them.

00:18:23.610 --> 00:18:25.210
Again, since they're coming from a place

00:18:25.210 --> 00:18:26.710
that isn't normal in the lung,

00:18:26.710 --> 00:18:30.390
so it's like coming from the
skin instead of the lung,

00:18:30.390 --> 00:18:31.500
these cells look different

00:18:31.500 --> 00:18:34.010
than most of the cells of the lung.

00:18:34.010 --> 00:18:35.170
And we can take advantage of that

00:18:35.170 --> 00:18:38.650
to actually find the specific cancer cells

00:18:38.650 --> 00:18:40.220
that are in the lung.

00:18:40.220 --> 00:18:41.053
So to do this,

00:18:41.053 --> 00:18:44.820
we use antibodies that can
identify these special proteins

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:45.923
on the cancer cells.

00:18:50.210 --> 00:18:51.870
So, yeah, these are antibodies,

00:18:53.080 --> 00:18:55.160
and then we use another antibody,

00:18:55.160 --> 00:18:56.520
called the secondary antibody,

00:18:56.520 --> 00:19:00.313
that has a tag on it.

00:19:01.590 --> 00:19:05.393
So just gonna use a different color here,

00:19:07.140 --> 00:19:08.620
and this tag is fluorescent,

00:19:08.620 --> 00:19:10.093
so it basically glows.

00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:15.980
And then we can use a microscope

00:19:15.980 --> 00:19:20.630
in order to basically shoot a laser at...

00:19:20.630 --> 00:19:22.380
Oops, accidentally stopped sharing.

00:19:26.270 --> 00:19:27.820
So this microscope can shoot...

00:19:29.090 --> 00:19:30.890
Let's see, I wanna change the color.

00:19:36.760 --> 00:19:38.230
So this microscope shoots a laser

00:19:38.230 --> 00:19:42.400
and it turns on this glowing fluorescence,

00:19:42.400 --> 00:19:44.787
then that fluorescence goes
back to the microscope,

00:19:44.787 --> 00:19:46.680
and it's able to show you

00:19:46.680 --> 00:19:50.783
where the antibody's
bound to the cancer cells.

00:19:54.270 --> 00:19:57.423
So to do that, basically,

00:20:00.640 --> 00:20:02.973
we cut up the lungs into these slides,

00:20:04.340 --> 00:20:07.790
and then we add on the
antibodies and everything

00:20:07.790 --> 00:20:08.963
in a process,

00:20:09.980 --> 00:20:10.813
and after about a day or so,

00:20:10.813 --> 00:20:13.300
then when we're ready
to actually look at them

00:20:13.300 --> 00:20:15.000
under the microscope.

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:17.600
So I'm actually in the room
now with the microscope.

00:20:20.618 --> 00:20:21.693
So I'm gonna show you guys.

00:20:30.790 --> 00:20:33.803
This is our big microscope.

00:20:37.810 --> 00:20:39.710
So this is actually where you look in.

00:20:41.540 --> 00:20:42.590
Actually, the part where you look at in

00:20:42.590 --> 00:20:45.260
is way smaller compared
to like the whole thing,

00:20:45.260 --> 00:20:47.360
which basically takes up this entire room.

00:20:51.140 --> 00:20:54.400
And then once you wanna save the pictures

00:20:54.400 --> 00:20:55.233
that we see in the microscope,

00:20:55.233 --> 00:21:00.233
then we have a computer here
that saves the pictures.

00:21:07.620 --> 00:21:09.890
How much do you guys
think that microscope is?

00:21:09.890 --> 00:21:11.590
So when I heard it, I was shocked.

00:21:13.120 --> 00:21:14.670
So you can just guess in the...

00:21:16.430 --> 00:21:17.880
And while you guys are
guessing how much it costs,

00:21:17.880 --> 00:21:18.910
I'll answer these questions

00:21:18.910 --> 00:21:21.180
about how do we slice them so thin.

00:21:21.180 --> 00:21:23.230
So there's a machine called the microtome

00:21:24.440 --> 00:21:27.340
that you pretty much put the...

00:21:27.340 --> 00:21:29.480
It's kind of like those meat slicers,

00:21:30.820 --> 00:21:32.280
if you've ever seen
like a deli meat slicer,

00:21:32.280 --> 00:21:34.460
it just makes those
like really thin slices,

00:21:34.460 --> 00:21:35.670
and the machine just like cuts it.

00:21:35.670 --> 00:21:37.570
It's kind of similar thing.

00:21:37.570 --> 00:21:38.878
So you can like program the machine

00:21:38.878 --> 00:21:41.840
to tell it how thin we
want the slices to be,

00:21:41.840 --> 00:21:42.770
and then put the lung in there,

00:21:42.770 --> 00:21:46.250
and it cuts the lungs.

00:21:46.250 --> 00:21:48.083
And in terms of the orientation,

00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:50.930
it doesn't really matter unless...

00:21:52.260 --> 00:21:55.110
'Cause usually whoever's doing it

00:21:55.110 --> 00:21:58.030
kind of places them in the same way.

00:21:58.030 --> 00:21:59.860
If you wanna look at a
certain part of the lung,

00:21:59.860 --> 00:22:03.160
then it is important to like
make sure you're orienting it

00:22:03.160 --> 00:22:03.993
in the machine,

00:22:03.993 --> 00:22:06.560
so that you can get the
part of the lung you want.

00:22:06.560 --> 00:22:09.699
- [Ms. Mason] Is the lung
encased in something harder?

00:22:09.699 --> 00:22:11.107
Oh, okay.

00:22:11.107 --> 00:22:13.010
Then they're squishing under a blade.

00:22:13.010 --> 00:22:14.220
- Yeah, yeah.

00:22:14.220 --> 00:22:18.110
After I took the lungs out and
put them in tubes like this,

00:22:18.110 --> 00:22:21.300
then we do a process
called paraffin embedding.

00:22:21.300 --> 00:22:25.110
So we basically put
them in this kind of...

00:22:26.681 --> 00:22:27.980
I don't know what the
most similar to like...

00:22:27.980 --> 00:22:29.070
Kind of a wax,

00:22:29.070 --> 00:22:31.050
basically a wax mold.

00:22:31.050 --> 00:22:33.910
And then from that you
can cut through the wax

00:22:33.910 --> 00:22:35.713
and take some lung with it as well.

00:22:36.860 --> 00:22:39.730
So you guys have made some good guesses.

00:22:39.730 --> 00:22:42.710
So the highest was $800,000.

00:22:42.710 --> 00:22:44.760
I just spoiled it, sorry, guys (laughs).

00:22:44.760 --> 00:22:46.150
The highest is $100,000.

00:22:46.150 --> 00:22:50.260
It actually cost $800,000
just for that microscope.

00:22:50.260 --> 00:22:53.220
So it's very expensive to do the science,

00:22:53.220 --> 00:22:57.720
and we spent a lot of money to
like figure these things out,

00:22:57.720 --> 00:22:58.560
and every time I come here,

00:22:58.560 --> 00:22:59.610
I'm scared that I'm gonna break it,

00:22:59.610 --> 00:23:01.640
because that'd be my whole,

00:23:01.640 --> 00:23:05.483
entire life's earnings, basically.

00:23:07.895 --> 00:23:09.860
Yeah, so I'm gonna show
you guys an example

00:23:09.860 --> 00:23:14.430
of a picture that we get
at the end of this process.

00:23:14.430 --> 00:23:19.430
And then we can do a bit
of Q and A at the end.

00:23:29.187 --> 00:23:33.000
So this is a picture I took
recently with that microscope.

00:23:35.576 --> 00:23:37.403
And you see the glowing.

00:23:38.870 --> 00:23:39.800
So I'm just gonna let it play,

00:23:39.800 --> 00:23:42.400
and then actually pause it
at kind of the main part.

00:23:43.450 --> 00:23:45.590
So this is me kind of going up and down

00:23:45.590 --> 00:23:46.580
through the section

00:23:48.230 --> 00:23:51.923
in order get the full dimensions
of the tumor in the lung.

00:23:53.340 --> 00:23:57.273
So I'll just pause it here.

00:24:01.820 --> 00:24:03.250
So, basically, in order to do this.

00:24:03.250 --> 00:24:04.650
So as I mentioned,

00:24:04.650 --> 00:24:06.340
we used these antibodies,

00:24:06.340 --> 00:24:08.030
in this case, it's glowing green,

00:24:08.030 --> 00:24:09.900
the cancer cells.

00:24:09.900 --> 00:24:12.900
We also need is a stain called DAPI,

00:24:12.900 --> 00:24:15.640
that just stains every cell in the lung.

00:24:15.640 --> 00:24:16.750
So it's not specific,

00:24:16.750 --> 00:24:19.890
but it kind of lets us know
that wherever we see green,

00:24:19.890 --> 00:24:20.723
we know it's real,

00:24:20.723 --> 00:24:22.500
because there's also a nucleus,

00:24:22.500 --> 00:24:24.820
or a blue dye with that green,

00:24:24.820 --> 00:24:27.250
because, you know, for example like here,

00:24:27.250 --> 00:24:28.760
it's possible to just
have a bunch of green,

00:24:28.760 --> 00:24:30.010
but it's not actually a cell,

00:24:30.010 --> 00:24:32.973
because there's no nucleus
associated with it.

00:24:34.420 --> 00:24:35.440
So this is kind of how,

00:24:35.440 --> 00:24:39.120
even though I can't see this tumor by eye,

00:24:39.120 --> 00:24:41.030
by using a microscope that can go

00:24:41.030 --> 00:24:43.170
from 10 times stronger than my eye

00:24:43.170 --> 00:24:45.310
to up to 100 times stronger than my eye,

00:24:45.310 --> 00:24:48.993
I can actually see in
these lungs now the tumors.

00:24:50.400 --> 00:24:55.010
And I'll do another picture
that kind of zooms in more.

00:24:55.010 --> 00:24:57.151
So this is at 40x,

00:24:57.151 --> 00:24:59.240
so 40 times stronger than my eye.

00:24:59.240 --> 00:25:00.980
And now you can see the
actual individual cells

00:25:00.980 --> 00:25:02.620
pretty clearly.

00:25:02.620 --> 00:25:05.170
So like this one's a pretty good one.

00:25:05.170 --> 00:25:09.120
Again, this green signifies
that it's a cancer cell.

00:25:09.120 --> 00:25:11.050
And then since there's blue in the middle,

00:25:11.050 --> 00:25:13.140
I know that it's a real cancer cell

00:25:13.140 --> 00:25:16.850
and not just the antibody going wherever.

00:25:16.850 --> 00:25:19.810
So maybe there's like one
more minute for questions,

00:25:19.810 --> 00:25:22.470
but if you guys have anything else

00:25:26.830 --> 00:25:28.350
you wanted to ask...

00:25:28.350 --> 00:25:30.280
I can also put my email in the chat

00:25:30.280 --> 00:25:32.370
if you wanted to ask about,

00:25:32.370 --> 00:25:37.093
you know, science or why
I chose to do an MD-PhD.

00:25:38.374 --> 00:25:39.490
- [Ms. Mason] I would love to hear about

00:25:39.490 --> 00:25:41.428
why you chose the MD-PhD.

00:25:41.428 --> 00:25:45.320
- Yeah, for sure.
- It seems to be really hard.

00:25:45.320 --> 00:25:46.760
- I was actually in high school as well

00:25:46.760 --> 00:25:49.740
when I kind of learned
about the MD-PhD process.

00:25:49.740 --> 00:25:50.670
So like in school,

00:25:50.670 --> 00:25:54.420
I just always loved science
more than any other subjects,

00:25:54.420 --> 00:25:57.023
and biology specifically.

00:25:58.284 --> 00:25:59.117
And my mom was a nurse,

00:25:59.117 --> 00:26:01.120
so she had like these anatomy books

00:26:01.120 --> 00:26:02.620
that I would look at,

00:26:02.620 --> 00:26:03.680
and things like that.

00:26:03.680 --> 00:26:07.110
And I would watch like medical
documentaries or whatever.

00:26:07.110 --> 00:26:10.130
So I was like, okay, I really,
really wanna be a doctor,

00:26:10.130 --> 00:26:13.113
but also really like the
discovery aspect of science.

00:26:14.160 --> 00:26:17.320
And I liked watching

00:26:17.320 --> 00:26:19.447
or like seeing like science documentaries

00:26:19.447 --> 00:26:20.820
and like hearing scientists talk about

00:26:20.820 --> 00:26:23.580
how they discovered this thing,

00:26:23.580 --> 00:26:25.040
or like the cellular process,

00:26:25.040 --> 00:26:27.030
or like how this...

00:26:27.030 --> 00:26:29.400
So I basically liked most aspects.

00:26:29.400 --> 00:26:32.360
I liked the idea of like
working with patients

00:26:32.360 --> 00:26:34.710
and like learning about the
human body and everything,

00:26:34.710 --> 00:26:38.440
but I also like the idea of being a person

00:26:38.440 --> 00:26:42.690
who like discovers certain
things that nobody knows yet.

00:26:42.690 --> 00:26:43.700
So I felt conflicted,

00:26:43.700 --> 00:26:46.220
so I was like, "Should I be a doctor?

00:26:46.220 --> 00:26:47.797
Or should I be a scientist?"

00:26:48.690 --> 00:26:49.840
So then I kind of like,

00:26:51.150 --> 00:26:53.050
just Googling what are
different career paths

00:26:53.050 --> 00:26:54.530
in science, whatever.

00:26:54.530 --> 00:26:57.070
Then I like stumbled upon MD-PhD,

00:26:57.070 --> 00:26:59.010
and like learned that it's
actually possible to do both.

00:26:59.010 --> 00:27:02.220
You can get an MD and
like go to med school

00:27:02.220 --> 00:27:03.053
to become a doctor,

00:27:03.053 --> 00:27:04.040
but you can also get a PhD

00:27:04.040 --> 00:27:06.230
and like learn how to be a scientist,

00:27:06.230 --> 00:27:08.380
and there are programs
that integrate that,

00:27:09.720 --> 00:27:12.860
so that you can pretty much
become a physician-scientist

00:27:12.860 --> 00:27:14.420
who like sees your patients

00:27:14.420 --> 00:27:17.100
and like goes to lab and
studies the same things

00:27:17.100 --> 00:27:20.200
that your patients are being affected by.

00:27:20.200 --> 00:27:24.750
And I liked being able to both
have that immediate impact

00:27:24.750 --> 00:27:26.160
with like actually working with people,

00:27:26.160 --> 00:27:28.050
and like giving them therapies,

00:27:28.050 --> 00:27:30.340
and like speaking with them one-on-one,

00:27:30.340 --> 00:27:32.390
but also liked kind of
the long-term impact

00:27:32.390 --> 00:27:34.710
that doing the research would have.

00:27:34.710 --> 00:27:36.370
So like in 10, 20 years,

00:27:36.370 --> 00:27:38.630
like the research I'm doing might actually

00:27:38.630 --> 00:27:40.830
have an effect on
patients in the long-term.

00:27:42.170 --> 00:27:44.940
So that's kind of why
I decided to do MD-PhD.

00:27:44.940 --> 00:27:46.180
It is a long process.

00:27:46.180 --> 00:27:49.210
So medical school normally is four years.

00:27:49.210 --> 00:27:51.540
And then you go on to
residency and fellowship,

00:27:51.540 --> 00:27:54.332
which was more training
after medical school.

00:27:54.332 --> 00:27:56.200
And the MD-PhD program is eight years,

00:27:56.200 --> 00:28:00.180
so pretty much double the time,

00:28:00.180 --> 00:28:03.150
because you have to fit in that PhD.

00:28:03.150 --> 00:28:04.520
So if you were to do them separately,

00:28:04.520 --> 00:28:05.450
if you were to do an MD,

00:28:05.450 --> 00:28:08.350
and decided, oh, I
actually wanna get a PhD,

00:28:08.350 --> 00:28:11.175
a PhD is on average like six years now.

00:28:11.175 --> 00:28:12.200
So if we were to do them separately,

00:28:12.200 --> 00:28:14.140
it'd be like 10 years.

00:28:14.140 --> 00:28:16.517
So like doing an MD-PhD
program does shorten it,

00:28:16.517 --> 00:28:18.117
but it's still like a long time,

00:28:19.256 --> 00:28:20.700
but one benefit is that,

00:28:20.700 --> 00:28:21.820
again, they're like integrated.

00:28:21.820 --> 00:28:24.760
So they're really teaching you career-wise

00:28:24.760 --> 00:28:27.270
how can you do these both as a career,

00:28:27.270 --> 00:28:29.260
become a physician-scientist,

00:28:29.260 --> 00:28:30.570
and also they pay for med school

00:28:30.570 --> 00:28:31.660
and they give you a stipend.

00:28:31.660 --> 00:28:33.980
So I don't think about it
as eight years of school.

